Native views: place a view in step with the frame that moves its hole, log the Metal layer's alpha, and keep hole and
view edges seamless. Shared by the Apple backends (macOS's upstream one, 0052's iOS).

What:
- gpui_apple `MetalRenderer::draw_placing_native_views(scene, places_native_views)` (`draw` passes false): a frame
  that moves, resizes, shows or hides a native view is presented with the Core Animation transaction that places the
  view (`presentsWithTransaction` for that frame only, as upstream's live resize does for every frame), so hole and
  view reach the screen together; every other frame presents on its own. It logs the layer's alpha mode
  (`metal layer alpha: opaque` / `premultiplied, native views show through`) each time a native view shows or goes.
- gpui_apple `native_view_placement.rs` (new, pure, table-tested on the host by scripts/patch-test.sh, `apple-pure`):
  macOS's `Placement` (moved out of upstream's window/native_views.rs, with its test as table rows) made shared with
  iOS by a `YAxis` (AppKit up, UIKit down), from core's `native_view_placement::visible` (0048: the element's bounds
  are whole device pixels already, so only the points conversion is here). Where the screen resamples the layer,
  core's `hole_inset(true)` cuts the hole one pixel inside the view, so the layer's own pixels cover the view's
  blended edges; `MetalRenderer::set_native_view_hole_inset` applies it (0 by default, so exact edges at 1x/2x/3x
  stay).
- macOS: `draw` passes `Layout::places_views()` (the scene moves, resizes, shows, hides or restacks a view); placement
  from gpui_apple,
  the background colour from core's `native_view_placement::background`.
- Who takes pointer input over a view (`NativeViewInput`, macOS `View`, iOS `Window`) is patch 0048's.

Why: upstream presents every frame on its own, so a view's new frame reaches the screen a frame or two before the
hole that moves with it (upstream's own comment in macOS's native_views.rs); the gap shows the window's background.
Measured on the iOS simulator (0052) with the view's slot moving every frame: 28 of 442 recorded frames showed the gap
colour presented on their own, 0 of 212 (and 0 of 228 after this revision) presented in step. Where the compositor
resamples (iOS Display Zoom, a rotation's turn), a hole and a view sharing an edge both blend it, which leaves a
one-pixel seam of the gap colour; the inset closes it. Idle frames are untouched, so a window whose native view alone
changes still renders 0 GPUI frames. macOS has no API for a scaled display mode's resampling: it passes no inset.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

